WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:02.767
-Our next guest is a talented
singer and song writer

00:00:02.767 --> 00:00:04.467
you know from
both his solo career

00:00:04.467 --> 00:00:05.901
and work with One Direction.

00:00:05.901 --> 00:00:08.133
His third solo album "The Show"

00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:10.467
will be out June and is
available for preorder now.

00:00:10.467 --> 00:00:13.133
He will also be a coach on the
new season of "The Voice,"

00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:15.534
which premieres March 6th
at 8:00 PM here on NBC.

00:00:15.534 --> 00:00:16.734
Let's take a look.

00:00:16.734 --> 00:00:19.200
-I nearly hit my button
on your first note.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.267
-Long enough for me to actually
figure it out before him.

00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:24.467
-Please, Kelly, come on.
Give me a chance.

00:00:24.467 --> 00:00:26.868
You remind me so much of
my good friend Maren Morris.

00:00:26.868 --> 00:00:28.601
-Oh, my gosh.
-I've spent a lot of time

00:00:28.601 --> 00:00:30.868
on the stage over the years
and become very, very, very

00:00:30.868 --> 00:00:32.267
fond of country music,

00:00:32.267 --> 00:00:34.868
and country always
leads into story telling,

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:36.734
and I tend to do that.

00:00:36.734 --> 00:00:39.734
Your voice, the control,
it was just unbelievable.

00:00:39.734 --> 00:00:41.167
I'm telling you,

00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:42.934
we're going to do big things
together if you come with me.

00:00:42.934 --> 00:00:45.767
[ Cheers and applause ]
Please!

00:00:45.767 --> 00:00:48.234
-Please welcome to the show
Niall Horan, everybody!

00:00:48.234 --> 00:00:51.234
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:51.234 --> 00:00:59.467
♪♪

00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:07.734
♪♪

00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:09.167
I'm so happy you're here,
my friend.

00:01:09.167 --> 00:01:11.167
-I'm happy to be here.
Huge fan of the show.

00:01:11.167 --> 00:01:13.167
-We were very lucky to --

00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:16.133
I was very lucky, I should say,
to work with you 10 years ago.

00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:19.300
You fellas were on the show,
and we did a sketch.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:22.400
I was on set for where Paul Rudd
was the number one.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.968
There we see Paul.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:25.968 --> 00:01:27.901
And I have spoken
for years about

00:01:27.901 --> 00:01:30.701
how you were
the nicest group of guys.

00:01:30.701 --> 00:01:32.434
And, I mean, you were
fairly young at the time.

00:01:32.434 --> 00:01:34.133
Was that an exciting thing
to do?

00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:37.133
-Oh, yeah.
It was crazy to be part of "SNL"

00:01:37.133 --> 00:01:39.334
on a few occasions is so cool.

00:01:39.334 --> 00:01:43.300
But that particular weekend
Paul was hosting the show.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.601
And the "Anchorman" crew
were there.

00:01:45.601 --> 00:01:47.033
-Yeah.
-They were promoting

00:01:47.033 --> 00:01:49.367
"Anchorman 2."
-You got to sing with the --

00:01:49.367 --> 00:01:51.868
and this is two of the great
casts of all time.

00:01:51.868 --> 00:01:56.701
-"Anchorman"
being like, no joke,

00:01:56.701 --> 00:01:58.133
my favorite movie of all time.

00:01:58.133 --> 00:02:02.801
And just be standing there
singing "Afternoon Delight"

00:02:02.801 --> 00:02:04.934
with that bunch of lunatics

00:02:04.934 --> 00:02:07.200
was pretty special.
-Yeah.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.334
The other funny thing about
the sketch with Paul I remember

00:02:09.334 --> 00:02:11.334
we had a great many extras

00:02:11.334 --> 00:02:13.367
who also
were big One Direction fans.

00:02:13.367 --> 00:02:16.434
And it was the least we ever had
to give direction

00:02:16.434 --> 00:02:19.000
to a group of young girls.
-"Applause. Applause. Applause."

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.701
They were already screaming.
-"When they walk out,

00:02:20.701 --> 00:02:22.133
like, lose your minds."

00:02:22.133 --> 00:02:23.934
They were like,
"I think we can do that."

00:02:23.934 --> 00:02:25.868
-Yeah, yeah.
"We can sort that out."

00:02:25.868 --> 00:02:27.667
-This is very exciting.
You have a new single out.

00:02:27.667 --> 00:02:29.267
-Yes, I do.
-You have a new album coming.

00:02:29.267 --> 00:02:30.467
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:02:30.467 --> 00:02:32.167
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:32.167 --> 00:02:33.968
-I think a lot of people --

00:02:33.968 --> 00:02:37.167
you know, a lot of artists
reacted different ways

00:02:37.167 --> 00:02:38.734
to the pandemic
and what it put them through.

00:02:38.734 --> 00:02:40.734
-Yeah.
-Did you feel like you wanted to

00:02:40.734 --> 00:02:42.334
start working on new music
right away?

00:02:42.334 --> 00:02:43.734
Did you give yourself
a little time?

00:02:43.734 --> 00:02:45.701
Because it's been a while since
you -- about three years?

00:02:45.701 --> 00:02:46.701
-Yeah, exactly.

00:02:46.701 --> 00:02:48.501
No, I kind of --
I released an album

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:52.100
the day we were told we were
going into lockdown.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:53.901
Some would say
that was great timing.

00:02:53.901 --> 00:02:55.367
[ Laughs ]

00:02:55.367 --> 00:02:57.133
No, but it was
one of those things

00:02:57.133 --> 00:02:59.601
where I release an album
and then that happened.

00:02:59.601 --> 00:03:01.400
And then the tour got cancelled.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:02.868
So I was thinking like...

00:03:02.868 --> 00:03:05.000
I was kind of just angry
at the world, like we all were.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:06.701
-Yeah.
-So, then I was sitting around,

00:03:06.701 --> 00:03:08.501
waiting for something to happen,

00:03:08.501 --> 00:03:10.234
waiting for
the creative juices to flow.

00:03:10.234 --> 00:03:11.934
Took a while.

00:03:11.934 --> 00:03:14.434
And then once it came,
it kind of --

00:03:14.434 --> 00:03:19.434
it opened the door for what is
now a finished product, record.

00:03:19.434 --> 00:03:21.868
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Very exciting.

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:23.267
I wonder, do you --

00:03:23.267 --> 00:03:24.534
I mean, obviously
you work on these songs

00:03:24.534 --> 00:03:27.434
and a lot of
post production on the music

00:03:27.434 --> 00:03:29.801
and finally get it to a place
where you're happy with it.

00:03:29.801 --> 00:03:32.400
And then it gets
released to the world.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:34.400
Are you someone who --

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.300
Do you sort of anticipate
the reaction to it?

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.701
Have you already moved on
to the next?

00:03:38.701 --> 00:03:42.667
-No, I'm just always so nervous.
Like, it's the biggest cliche.

00:03:42.667 --> 00:03:44.801
When ever you work on something,

00:03:44.801 --> 00:03:47.300
you just obviously want it
to do well.

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:48.868
But we have no idea.

00:03:48.868 --> 00:03:50.400
Especially these days,
it's hard to know --

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:51.767
it's so competitive out there.

00:03:51.767 --> 00:03:54.667
But I've got --
as you can hear, I have --

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:58.467
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:58.467 --> 00:04:01.968
I'm lucky enough to have
a strong, solid fan base.

00:04:01.968 --> 00:04:04.834
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:04.834 --> 00:04:07.200
Having said that -- Having said
that, you still don't know.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.601
So I'm just, like, after three
years of not releasing music,

00:04:09.601 --> 00:04:11.667
there's that, like,
"Please do good."

00:04:11.667 --> 00:04:13.968
-Yeah.
-But the reaction to

00:04:13.968 --> 00:04:15.901
this single first of all
has been ridiculous,

00:04:15.901 --> 00:04:17.467
which makes me feel
a lot better about the record.

00:04:17.467 --> 00:04:18.901
-Well, there you go.
Very exciting.

00:04:18.901 --> 00:04:20.400
And now this is really exciting,
'cause you talked about

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:21.634
your last tour being cancelled.

00:04:21.634 --> 00:04:23.400
You're going on the road
this summer

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:25.567
and you're doing
a great many festivals,

00:04:25.567 --> 00:04:27.501
which is different that

00:04:27.501 --> 00:04:30.033
sort of doing more
sort of conventional tour.

00:04:30.033 --> 00:04:31.901
Are you excited about
festival life?

00:04:31.901 --> 00:04:33.901
-I think that is the main thing
I wanted to do

00:04:33.901 --> 00:04:36.067
when I was finishing off
the record.

00:04:36.067 --> 00:04:38.167
I said to everyone, like,
"I would love to just do

00:04:38.167 --> 00:04:39.334
a summer of festivals,"

00:04:39.334 --> 00:04:40.868
especially having spent
so much time away.

00:04:40.868 --> 00:04:43.567
It's good to get back
in front of people in that way.

00:04:43.567 --> 00:04:45.033
You know, I can go
all over the world,

00:04:45.033 --> 00:04:47.334
and we're doing them
all over Europe and Japan

00:04:47.334 --> 00:04:48.934
and a couple here in the states.

00:04:48.934 --> 00:04:52.100
And, like, it's just going to be
good to feel the love.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:54.968
And I'm a huge festival goer
myself.

00:04:54.968 --> 00:04:56.701
I've been that drunk guy...

00:04:56.701 --> 00:04:58.801
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Yeah.

00:04:58.801 --> 00:05:00.667
-...standing in my welly boots

00:05:00.667 --> 00:05:03.667
in a field going,
"Who's that up there?

00:05:03.667 --> 00:05:05.234
I like the sound of that."

00:05:05.234 --> 00:05:07.467
So I want --
That's why I wanted to do it.

00:05:07.467 --> 00:05:08.701
I can get some new fans here,
Seth.

00:05:08.701 --> 00:05:10.234
-Well, absolutely.

00:05:10.234 --> 00:05:12.267
This speaks to the level
of difficulty of a festival.

00:05:12.267 --> 00:05:13.968
Because obviously, you know,
you have a group of people

00:05:13.968 --> 00:05:15.567
who are all music fans.
-Mm-hmm.

00:05:15.567 --> 00:05:17.200
-But, you know, specifically

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:19.501
they might be less there
to see you.

00:05:19.501 --> 00:05:21.501
Like, they might be there
to see the person before you

00:05:21.501 --> 00:05:23.767
or after you,
so you do have to win them over.

00:05:23.767 --> 00:05:25.801
And I think
it's really impressive that

00:05:25.801 --> 00:05:27.200
you want to take on
that challenge.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:28.667
-Oh, for sure.
-You don't necessarily have to,

00:05:28.667 --> 00:05:30.133
but I think that will be
so exciting.

00:05:30.133 --> 00:05:32.400
-No, it's a great way
of getting, like, new people.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:36.200
As you say, there's a solid
group there, but it's --

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:39.501
You know, I'd love to invite
more people into the world.

00:05:39.501 --> 00:05:42.501
-Do you feel like you get to
meet and hang out

00:05:42.501 --> 00:05:44.000
with other bands
in the festival world?

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.901
Is that one of the up sides?
-Yeah, no. They're always good.

00:05:47.901 --> 00:05:49.601
You meet people
that you have no idea

00:05:49.601 --> 00:05:51.100
you had anything in common with,

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:53.734
And before you know it's 2:00.
[ Laughs ]

00:05:53.734 --> 00:05:55.133
It's been a good night.
-Yeah?

00:05:55.133 --> 00:05:56.534
[ Laughter ]
And Europe,

00:05:56.534 --> 00:05:58.801
is it buses around Europe
when you're doing...?

00:05:58.801 --> 00:06:02.334
-Yeah. There's a lot of --
Because they're so far apart,

00:06:02.334 --> 00:06:04.534
other countries, you do have to
do a lot of flying.

00:06:04.534 --> 00:06:06.601
When we're in the states,
it's a lot of buses.

00:06:06.601 --> 00:06:08.467
-Do you enjoy -- Is bus life --

00:06:08.467 --> 00:06:10.534
Because I feel there's something
a little romantic about it

00:06:10.534 --> 00:06:12.734
maybe for the first three days.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:06:12.734 --> 00:06:14.868
You're like,
you've seen "Spinal Tap" twice

00:06:14.868 --> 00:06:18.100
and now you think
"Yeah, that's how it's done."

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:19.300
[ Laughs ]

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:22.834
Then it's like 105 degrees
in Houston, Texas.

00:06:22.834 --> 00:06:25.868
First week of July,
and it's sweaty

00:06:25.868 --> 00:06:27.868
and you've woken up and
air-conditioning is broken.

00:06:27.868 --> 00:06:30.834
It's just like, "Maybe not as
romantic as I thought."

00:06:30.834 --> 00:06:32.567
[ Laughter ]

00:06:32.567 --> 00:06:35.467
-Um, one place that's very well
air-conditioned,

00:06:35.467 --> 00:06:37.267
I've heard, is this studio
for "The Voice."

00:06:37.267 --> 00:06:39.534
Congratulations on that gig.
-It's Baltic.

00:06:39.534 --> 00:06:41.167
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:41.167 --> 00:06:44.400
-Is this --
How did this come to pass?

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:45.868
I'm assuming this is a situation

00:06:45.868 --> 00:06:48.400
where your phone just rang
one day and they reached out?

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:53.501
-Literally. Phone call from
the producers, Audrey, Kyra.

00:06:53.501 --> 00:06:55.968
And they just said,
"Before you even say anything,

00:06:55.968 --> 00:06:57.234
just come in and meet us."

00:06:57.234 --> 00:06:58.667
And they explained
how the show works

00:06:58.667 --> 00:07:00.868
and all that good stuff.

00:07:00.868 --> 00:07:03.400
And I was like,
"Yeah, let's just give it a go.

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:04.667
See what happens."

00:07:04.667 --> 00:07:06.734
I've been on a show like that.
-Of course, yeah.

00:07:06.734 --> 00:07:09.300
-So, I un-- I do...

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:12.367
I understand --
I understand how it works.

00:07:12.367 --> 00:07:14.300
But, yeah,
it was just a challenge,

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:15.701
something that
I wasn't expecting.

00:07:15.701 --> 00:07:17.968
-Right.
-You know, a great time to do it

00:07:17.968 --> 00:07:19.801
before I've got
a record coming out.

00:07:19.801 --> 00:07:22.300
And you're going to be
sick of my faces.

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:25.501
Or sick of my face.

00:07:25.501 --> 00:07:27.167
But, yeah, no,
it's been so fun.

00:07:27.167 --> 00:07:30.901
We were very lucky with the crew
of coaches that I'm with.

00:07:30.901 --> 00:07:32.901
-Yeah.
-It could've been horrific.

00:07:32.901 --> 00:07:34.968
You know, you're sitting there
every night pretending that

00:07:34.968 --> 00:07:36.801
you like the person
you're sat next to.

00:07:36.801 --> 00:07:40.367
I got very lucky
in Blake Shelton alone.

00:07:40.367 --> 00:07:41.367
-Yeah.
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:41.367 --> 00:07:43.000
-Now, I would imagine too

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:45.133
having gone through a show
like this, you must have --

00:07:45.133 --> 00:07:46.801
because obviously
it looks very exciting

00:07:46.801 --> 00:07:48.267
what we see on television.

00:07:48.267 --> 00:07:50.734
But you obviously are in this
situation you can have empathy

00:07:50.734 --> 00:07:52.100
with them
for what they're going through.

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:54.868
Because I think we all maybe
forget how stressful it is

00:07:54.868 --> 00:07:56.367
for an unknown artist to --

00:07:56.367 --> 00:07:57.734
especially in a room like that

00:07:57.734 --> 00:07:59.534
to be in front of
four very accomplished,

00:07:59.534 --> 00:08:00.701
very famous singers.
-Yeah.

00:08:00.701 --> 00:08:02.634
-They must feel nice knowing

00:08:02.634 --> 00:08:04.734
and being like, "Oh, I know
Niall has been on this side."

00:08:04.734 --> 00:08:06.567
-Whoo!
-I guess that's a part --

00:08:06.567 --> 00:08:08.167
Ooh, That was a big one.

00:08:08.167 --> 00:08:09.234
[ Laughter ]

00:08:09.234 --> 00:08:10.667
No, it is -- It's good...

00:08:10.667 --> 00:08:12.701
I think that's
how I got half of my team

00:08:12.701 --> 00:08:15.701
because they understand that
I've got that empathy with them.

00:08:15.701 --> 00:08:19.334
I was -- You know,
17-, 18-, 19-year-old people

00:08:19.334 --> 00:08:21.534
coming on here,
and I was that 16-year-old

00:08:21.534 --> 00:08:23.567
just kind of knowing
that person there

00:08:23.567 --> 00:08:25.334
has got my future
in their hands.

00:08:25.334 --> 00:08:28.033
And it's -- That's the hardest
thing about "The Voice."

00:08:28.033 --> 00:08:30.634
It's like I have to make
in-game decisions

00:08:30.634 --> 00:08:33.133
over two singers that
are standing in front of me...

00:08:33.133 --> 00:08:36.567
knowing that I have
their future in my hands.

00:08:36.567 --> 00:08:39.434
And that's the part that I feel
like the empathy side of me.

00:08:39.434 --> 00:08:41.133
I hope I don't lose it,
because I would be heartless

00:08:41.133 --> 00:08:43.400
if I did lose that.
-Well, I think if you want to

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:45.534
know what it's like
to be without empathy,

00:08:45.534 --> 00:08:46.968
that feels like probably what
Blake Shelton does, right?

00:08:46.968 --> 00:08:48.501
-Blake Shelton. There you go.

00:08:48.501 --> 00:08:52.868
After 12 years...
12 years and 23 seasons.

00:08:52.868 --> 00:08:55.100
-Yeah.
-He's stone cold, that man.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:57.767
-He's stone cold. A man
who this is his last season.

00:08:57.767 --> 00:09:00.801
Obviously he's done a great
amount of success on the show.

00:09:00.801 --> 00:09:02.534
When ever he's here,

00:09:02.534 --> 00:09:05.334
he is not shy about
bragging about his success.

00:09:05.334 --> 00:09:06.868
Has he been giving you
a hard time?

00:09:06.868 --> 00:09:08.467
Because he also seems
very good at that.

00:09:08.467 --> 00:09:12.300
-That's all he does anywhere, to
everyone that will listen to him

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:14.400
or don't want to listen to him.
-[ Laughs ]

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:16.801
-He's just constantly
just nailing everyone.

00:09:16.801 --> 00:09:19.434
-He's a very charismatic
and affable bully.

00:09:19.434 --> 00:09:22.901
-Yeah. [ Chuckles ] Correct.
That's exactly what he is.

00:09:22.901 --> 00:09:24.434
But he's an absolute beauty.

00:09:24.434 --> 00:09:27.000
And, you know, he's going
to be missed around that show.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:30.300
Like, he is "The Voice" --
probably in his head, too.

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:32.667
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:32.667 --> 00:09:35.868
-But he's so good to work with.

00:09:35.868 --> 00:09:37.467
And, yeah, he'll be missed
around there for sure.

00:09:37.467 --> 00:09:39.100
-I mean, yeah, I think one of
the reasons the show

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:41.801
is so exceptional
is the connection

00:09:41.801 --> 00:09:43.501
you guys all have together
and the chemistry.

00:09:43.501 --> 00:09:46.367
Do you feel like --
When he leaves,

00:09:46.367 --> 00:09:48.767
do you feel like you'll have
a good Blake Shelton impression?

00:09:48.767 --> 00:09:50.434
Do you have one yet?
-[ As Blake ] I'm Blake Shelton.

00:09:50.434 --> 00:09:52.801
Uh...
[ Laughter ]

00:09:52.801 --> 00:09:54.434
This is my...

00:09:54.434 --> 00:09:56.234
[ Normal voice ] I feel like a
politician every time I do it.

00:09:56.234 --> 00:09:59.167
[ Blake ] This is my last season
on "The Voice,"

00:09:59.167 --> 00:10:05.434
and I would love if you would be
on the last-ever Team Blake.

00:10:05.434 --> 00:10:07.601
[ Normal voice ] And he says
that to every single person

00:10:07.601 --> 00:10:11.000
who comes out --
win, lose or draw.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:14.534
-I will tell you, I know you
said you felt like a politician,

00:10:14.534 --> 00:10:15.834
but you said that
and looked at me,

00:10:15.834 --> 00:10:17.200
I was like,
"I want to be on Team Blake."

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:19.734
It totally worked.
It was very convincing.

00:10:19.734 --> 00:10:21.968
-I need a catch phrase.
-You've got to work on it.

00:10:21.968 --> 00:10:24.033
Hey, man, what a pleasure
to have you here.

00:10:24.033 --> 00:10:25.200
I really can't thank you enough.

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:27.267
And congratulations
on the new music.

00:10:27.267 --> 00:10:28.667
Niall Horan.

00:10:28.667 --> 00:10:30.133
His new album
"The Show"

00:10:30.133 --> 00:10:32.400
is available for preorder now,

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:34.033
and "The Voice" premieres
March 6th at 8:00 PM

00:10:34.033 --> 00:10:35.234
right here on NBC.

00:10:35.234 --> 00:10:36.300
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

